>even more harmful (logs cannot be written any more etc) than a / 
>overflow.

I'd expect the main advantage is that /var will have more
free space than a typical root.  Actually, many /var file
systems will more free than roots have space at all, free
and otherwise.  The extra free space will reduce the chances
that the application will cause a problem, unless it happens
to be very anti-social.  If it is anti-social, then it will
eventually use up all the free space of whatever file system
it is on.

