answer is YES.  The ls command uses lstat(2) to obtain file information
including time.  The full time values are stored in st_mtime (seconds
since 1970) and st_umtime (microseconds).

You could write a program to extract and display the time values if you
need more precision than is available via the ls command.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>


main( int argc, char **argv )
{
    struct stat st;

    while( argv[1] != NULL ) {
        if ( lstat( argv[1], &st ) != 0 ) {
            perror( argv[1] );
            exit( EXIT_FAILURE );
        }
        printf( "%s: %s", argv[1], ctime( &st.st_mtime ) );
        argv++;
    }
}


