Indeed EV7 is a 2002 product. There may be samples this year, but I wouldn't
expect to see EV7 in systems until late next year at the earliest. In the
interim there will be 1GHz (I have a couple of these in my hot little hands)
Alpha EV68s in a couple of months, followed by ~1.2GHz and faster parts. As
Shannon Knows Compaq subscribers know, there's also an EV69 part on the
roadmap.

Where EV7 will shine is in large MP configurations. Thanks to the on-chip
switching infrastructure (and only Compaq knows what else), EV7 will permit
large mesh-architecture MP systems with extremely low latency. Plus there's
the lockstepping support for Himalaya NSK systems.

T'is correct that advanced chips tend to be easier touted than implemented.
EV6 was a tad late. UltraSparc III was about three years late (and is it
shipping in volume? who knows?). And then there's Unobtainium.

P4 definitely has Alpha trounced in the MHz race. At 1.7GHz, P4 must be
twice as good as an 833MHz Alpha. Of course, if such is the case, P4 will
likely be twice as good as a first-generation IA64. Intel may have screwed
the pooch by constantly stressing MHz over actual performance.




