I reckon Charlie's sense of irony is still in recovery (Betty Ford's joint,
wasn't it? ;-)

Wildfire was supposed to stuff 256 (then 128 - then 64) processors into one
"system". They committed to the first number to some pretty big "entities". An
Alpha SC in a "box" (big box, sure, but one box, no clustering required).

Didn't happen, of course. Wildfire had one hell of a burn rate going thru the
three-year slip, and eventually ended up with a 32p box for a hefty list
price, with higher latency/lower bw memory than their smaller boxen. That had
to cost them big, considering the promises broken to government and industry
alike...

Meanwhile the "volume server" team delivers DS40/Clipper, DS20, DS10L/Slate,
and soon DS45/Privateer, claims all kinds of significant wins (the 1000p farm
at Celera & the 640p farm at LL were just the beginning), and was clearly the
money pump that kept the dollars pouring into Wildfire and Marvel (aka
"Aquarius 2 and 3") plus some cash to help cover up Q's tanking peecee
bidness...

Yet there was virtually no effort expended to keep that development group
alive. 

Root cause: the same old management team still believes in big, expensive
iron, even if they miss their FCS date by two or three years while spending
prodigious bucks missing target after target...

Seems pretty ironic from here...and a damned shame. And does anyone really
think API is up to taking that space on?

btw: Ask Charlie what happens to Compaq's Alpha platform development now that
there's no chance of hitting any of the incentive programs on Marvel. Think
they'll just move the goalposts back - again?

