systems.
> What distinguished their PCs from anyone else?

In the beginning ;-} CPQ (which of course stands for COMPAtible Quality)
offered a high-quality, popularly-priced alternative to IBM peecees. One of
the first portables, too, if you consider a 38-pound suitcase to be
portable.

If you look at some of CPQ's early PCs, they were more like Rainbows than
IBM PC/AT and Leading Edge and Hyundai peecees. Well built. Quality
components. Gold-plated edge connectors. Decent MTBFs, etc.


CPQ then in the late 80's begat Wintel servers, and promised to take on the
minicomputers. Which they successfully did. The DECasaur and other denizens
of the minicomputer Jurassic Park didn't know how to deal with this, so they
became fossils.

As for CPQ, it made a name for itself and leveraged that name. Marketing in
the peecee space is different, and arguably much easier, than marketing in
the enterprise space.

The problem I believe CPQ faces now is learning how to market effectively to
enterprise customers. Many of the Big Dogs at CPQ are Wintel-centric, and
apparently maintain a Wintel-centric marketing philosophy. The Rich
Marcellos and Pauline Nists and Kent Fersons and Don Harberts thus have a
problem. While all of the aforementioned know their platforms and the
differentiation they deliver, articulating marketing messages that leverage
these differentiators is difficult indeed when such messages must be passed
through a Wintel lens.





