Other than the "mine's bigger" factor the speed of the high end CPUs has
become of limited consequence for the broad market.  Few applications
require all the CPU power that even the current generation of CPUs provide.
Back to the dreaded car analogy, I'm reasonably certain somebody (Ford, for
instance) could sell me a 1000 horsepower motor, but that much power is
simply not required in the typical car or truck.  And the fact that Ford
could produce such a motor is completely irrelevant to me when the motor I
will be buying requires no more than 1/5th as much power - and is available
from at least 20 manufacturers.  (Ie, I'm not going to buy a Ford because 
they can build something "ultra" that I'll never use.)  Now, if I needed a
motor for a train or some other industrial application the huge motor might
be a good selling point.  However, I'd bet that nobody here has a 1000 HP 
motor in any of their vehicles.

My point being that the "ultra" performance of the next Alpha, if it
actually ever does arrive, will be just as irrelevant to the broad market
as is the performance of the current Alpha.  Case in point - we're shutting
down our VMS system and migrating most users to a slower (for the CPU) Sun
system because other factors are much more important than raw CPU power. 
Software avialability being the biggest of these factors.

