The problem with Alpha has always be one of system 
balance and this does not seem to be being addressed.

The Alpha 8400/GS140 suffered for most of its life
for not having enough memory or I/O bandwidth, for
having a realtively long latency memory subsystem 
and for having to trade I/O/Memory/CPU slots. This
ment that the 8400 consistently got trashed on 
almost any benchmark you choose to pick (except
 SPECint and SPECfp) by better balanced machines 
which generally had slower CPU's as measured by
SPECint and SPECfp.

The GS160 and GS320 seem to suffer from exactly 
the same problem. They get trashed on almost any 
benchmark you choose to pick (except SPECint and
SPECfp) by better balanced machines. Sadly the 
only difference being that at the very beginning
of its product life the 8400 was a competitive 
machine from a performance standpoint while the
same could not be said to be true for the 
WildFire.

Single CPU SPECint/SPECfp are almost completely 
irrelevant measures of performance for large SMP
servers and the apparently mindless concentration 
on these two measures by Alpha protagonists is 
just symptomatic of the Alpha illness.

