In article <qIsB6.3628$zv6.2622115@typhoon2.ba-dsg.net>,
MrSignor <MrSignor@nospam_bellatlantic.net> wrote:
>Is there a reliable way (across Unix platforms) to wake-up a thread
>sleeping/waiting on a Select or Poll, that is not timing out the request.
>
>Basically, I have a thread, waiting for work on 1-n sockets. Upon
>occassion, I would like to wake the sleeping-thread, because I
>need to bring something to it's attention.

Um...  Maybe I'm missing something obvious, but why not write to one of
the sockets it's listening to?

Perhaps you could use a pipe.  When you want the thread to wake up and
do whatever, just write one byte to the pipe, using a non-blocking
write.  If you succeed in writing, the poll()-ing/select()-ing process
will get the byte.  If you don't succeed in writing, that means another
byte is already in the pipe waiting to be read, which is just as good
anyway, since the bytes are just random garbage designed to cause the
thread to wake up.

  - Logan
